{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"11e61c548455de5e","name":"card_event_rationing","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Homefront/events/card_event_rationing","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"RATIONING","text":"Choose One - Remove Bond from cards in your hand OR give your HQ +4 defense.","flavorText":"After the US entered WWII, the government introduced a large-scale rationing system to manage critical shortages and support the war effort. Overseen by the Office of Price Administration, rationing began with tires in early 1942 and soon expanded to include gasoline, sugar, coffee, meat and canned goods. More than 5,000 local boards, staffed mostly by volunteers, issued ration books to civilians, who exchanged stamps for limited quantities of goods. Some stamps featured symbols like airplanes or wheat but they did not always specify which item they applied to, which was later clarified in newspapers. The rationing system aimed to ensure fair distribution and prioritize military needs, with exemptions made for some industries or individuals with essential jobs. Housewives became key players in conserving food, encouraged to cook with substitutes and avoid waste. Victory gardens and “Meatless Mondays” became symbols of homefront patriotism. While the program faced complaints and black-market activity, it was generally respected and helped the US sustain a long and resource-consuming global conflict.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_rationing","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_rationing_title","SourceString":"RATIONING","LocalizedString":"RATIONING"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_rationing_text","SourceString":"Choose One - Remove Bond from cards in your hand OR give your HQ +4 defense.","LocalizedString":"Choose One - Re